[
  {
    "id": "CV-001",
    "title": "Verify local Codex transport and thread ownership",
    "type": "chore",
    "status": "open",
    "priority": "medium",
    "blocks": ["CV-002", "CV-003"],
    "area": "codex-connection",
    "description": "Establish the runtime contract needed to ask questions from generated flow-map HTML. Official Codex App Server docs expose thread/start, thread/resume, turn/start and event streaming, but access to an arbitrary existing host conversation has not been verified. Scope: prototype an authenticated local connection, dedicated-thread creation/resume, workspace binding, approvals/denials, interruption, reconnect and process cleanup; separately assess existing host-session ownership and compatibility. Acceptance: record tested CLI versions, reproducible commands and observed outcomes, select the supported transport, and update docs/planning/flow-map-codex-questions-plan.md before implementation. Task: docs/planning/tasks/001-codex-connection-spike.md. This planning PR does not complete the spike."
  },
  {
    "id": "CV-002",
    "title": "Ask Codex from a selected flow-map node",
    "type": "feature",
    "status": "open",
    "priority": "medium",
    "blocks": ["CV-003"],
    "area": "flow-map-questions",
    "description": "Let readers ask questions beside a selected node, step or issue and receive streamed Codex answers in a persistent dedicated map conversation. Depends on CV-001. Scope: optional local service, explicit question submission with selected map context and source references, visible repository/thread destination, streamed answers/tool status, approval handling, follow-ups after reload, and clear failure/recovery states. Acceptance: demonstrate context reaching the intended thread, a streamed answer and follow-up, permission denial and cancellation, authenticated origin/workspace boundaries, and the standalone viewer working without the service; document setup. Plan: docs/planning/flow-map-codex-questions-plan.md. Task: docs/planning/tasks/002-contextual-codex-questions.md. Keep connection credentials out of generated HTML."
  },
  {
    "id": "CV-003",
    "title": "Route map questions to a selected existing Codex thread",
    "type": "feature",
    "status": "open",
    "priority": "medium",
    "blocks": [],
    "area": "codex-connection",
    "description": "Support the user's choice to continue an existing Codex conversation from the map when its owning runtime exposes a compatible interface. Depends on CV-001 and CV-002. Scope: explicit thread/workspace selection, verified attach/resume behavior, correct response routing and busy-turn handling, and clear unavailable-session feedback. Acceptance: prove a question and reply are appended to the selected original thread with history and permissions preserved; test busy, missing and inaccessible destinations. Offer a dedicated conversation explicitly when attachment is unsupported, never silently create a new thread or claim same-session continuity. Plan: docs/planning/flow-map-codex-questions-plan.md. Task: docs/planning/tasks/003-existing-thread-routing.md. Keep this ticket open if existing-session access remains unverified or unsupported unless its scope is explicitly revised."
  },
  {
    "id": "CV-004",
    "title": "Render Markdown excerpts as formatted text in flow-map",
    "type": "feature",
    "status": "done",
    "priority": "medium",
    "blocks": [],
    "area": "flow-map-viewer",
    "description": "Excerpts of Markdown files appear as raw monospaced source: headings keep their # marks, tables show pipes and separator rows, and long prose lines run past the pane behind a scroll that is hidden until hover, so mapped docs are hard to read. Subtask 1: draw excerpts of .md files, and node and file notes, as formatted text (headings, paragraphs, lists, tables, quotes, fenced code, inline code, emphasis, http(s) links) from the builder's redacted lines, keeping the real file line numbers in a gutter and the highlighted issue line. Acceptance: node, step-file and issue excerpts of .md files render as formatted text and other files are unchanged; raw HTML, scripts, images and non-http(s) links in the source stay inert text; redaction runs before parsing; the builder ignores a supplied doc; the page makes no network request. Task: docs/planning/tasks/004-markdown-excerpts.md. Done 2026-10-11: 24 tests cover the parser, ranges that start inside a fenced block, notes, supplied blocks being ignored and redaction before parsing; a real 28-view research map rendered 452 excerpts with no leftover markup; a Chromium check of a docs map at 1440px and a narrow viewport showed headings, tables, lists and fenced code as formatted text with line numbers; a hostile Markdown page produced no live elements, event attributes or requests."
  },
  {
    "id": "CV-005",
    "title": "Clarify flow-map chart colours and emphasise the page header",
    "type": "fix",
    "status": "done",
    "priority": "medium",
    "blocks": [],
    "area": "flow-map-viewer",
    "description": "Two readability defects in the viewer's styling. Evidence: doc nodes draw as solid black boxes because --c-doc is never defined; edge wires and node outlines sit at 1.2:1 against their lane (3:1 is the non-text minimum); inactive node text falls to 1.8:1 to 2.5:1; kind colours repeat severity colours (data and question share one hex, user and risk are 1.4 apart and api and smell 4.3 apart in light and identical in dark, against a validator floor of 15); and the header is plain text on the page background. Subtask 1: chart colour. Define the missing doc colour, give wires and outlines at least 3:1, keep inactive text at least 4.5:1, and give each kind family a distinct hue that passes the data-viz palette checks in light and dark. Subtask 2: header emphasis. Give the title and subtitle a clearer block without adding content. Acceptance: no node renders black; the listed contrast pairs meet their minimums in both themes; no two hue families fall below the validator floors and no kind colour is closer than 9 to a severity colour under normal vision; the header reads as the first level of the page in light, dark and a 390px viewport. Task: docs/planning/tasks/005-chart-colour-and-header.md. Done 2026-10-11: wires and outlines are 3.8:1 light and 4.2:1 dark against the lane (were 1.2:1 and 1.35:1) and inactive node text keeps full ink and muted contrast; no node renders black in a docs map checked in light, dark and 390px; the six hue families pass the data-viz palette validator all-pairs in both themes (worst normal-vision 15.9 light and 15.1 dark, worst colour-blind 8.2 and 8.3, contrast at least 3.7:1); the nearest kind to a severity colour is 9.0 under normal vision (was 0.0), and the one pair below the colour-blind floor, queue gold against risk orange at 4.7, also differs by shape and icon; the header is checked in light, dark and 390px. Issue cards also keep their sentences in text ink and show severity on the icon."
  },
  {
    "id": "CV-006",
    "title": "Add a light and dark theme toggle button to the flow-map header",
    "type": "feature",
    "status": "done",
    "priority": "medium",
    "blocks": [],
    "area": "flow-map-viewer",
    "description": "The viewer follows the OS light or dark setting and gives the reader no way to change it from the page, so switching needs DevTools or an edit to the HTML. Subtask 1: add one header button that toggles between light and dark, starting from the OS setting, with no auto state and no stored preference. Acceptance: one button whose accessible name states the action; it flips the whole page, the chart and the details pane included, between the existing token sets; it works when the OS is light and when it is dark; its icon matches the action and it is reachable by keyboard; the page gains no dependency or network request. Task: docs/planning/tasks/006-theme-toggle.md. Done 2026-10-11: checked in Chromium with the OS set to light and to dark; one click flips the page, chart and details pane, the label and icon name the theme the next click switches to, an OS change before the first click updates the icon, the button is a native focusable control and sits clear of the title at 390px, and the page makes no network request. A build test asserts one toggle and both icons."
  }
]
